    "body": "The future of European airpower is very much up in the air, but it is clear that unmanned capabilities will play a major role going forward.\nIn this fireside chat, Breaking Defense Editor-in-Chief Aaron Mehta sits down with Dave Alexander, the president of General Atomics Aeronautical Systems, to discuss his views on the European market, what kind of industrial partnerships he looks for, and what a European CCA may do differently than an American one.\nYou can find the previous episodes of our European airpower roundtable here and at Breaking Defense Europe .\nBreaking Defense Video\nHow General Atomics views Europe’s unmanned future\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nA more flexible way to defend maneuver forces from drones that doesn’t tie them down [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nWhat’s haunting the Pentagon this Halloween?\nWatch Now »\npresented by\nThe new must-have capabilities for drones [Sponsored]\nWatch Now »\nArmy’s tech chief talks autonomy, acquisition and the future of drones\nWatch Now »\nDrones take over AUSA, plus the Army’s plan to bring in new vendors\nWatch Now »\nHegseth talks autonomy at AUSA, a key show focus\nWatch Now »\nArmy doubles down on autonomy, stakes out Indo-Pacific role: AUSA Day 2\nWatch Now »\nTelle talks ‘manufacturing rodeo,’ supply chain ‘weak links’: AUSA Day 1\nWatch Now »\nNew leaders, new drone command: What we expect from AUSA 2026\nWatch Now »\nScroll for more video",
